module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.bare_exit_124_read_as_an_observed_timeout_event

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data bare_exit_124_read_as_an_observed_timeout_event: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "bare_exit_124_read_as_an_observed_timeout_event" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "**A reported exit status of 124 does not establish that a wall-clock bound fired, and classifying it as an observed timeout kill asserts an observation the classifier does not hold** (INVALID STATE: GNU coreutils `timeout` returns 124 when it kills a command at its deadline AND otherwise returns the managed command's own status, so a child that simply exits 124 is indistinguishable from a deadline expiry. A classifier that receives only an exit code and a reason string has no timeout event to read, and rendering one is the fabricated-plausible-output failure DESIGN section 5 forbids.)",
    "EXECUTED COUNTEREXAMPLE, GNU coreutils 9.7, both arms run to completion: `timeout --kill-after=1s 5s sh -c 'exit 124'` returned 124 in about 6 ms with empty stdout and stderr and NO deadline expiry; `timeout --kill-after=1s 0.1s sleep 2` returned 124 in about 111 ms and is a genuine expiry. Identical status, identical empty output, two different events. Only elapsed time separates them here, and elapsed time is precisely the arm gunbc.memory_stall_refusal memory_stall_signal_choice_note refuses, because a timeout labelled pressure-detection refuses the honest slow case it exists to admit.",
    "THE SHARPER FORM, AND IT IS WHY THIS ROW EXISTS RATHER THAN A CLASSIFIER ARM. On the CI floor path there is no GNU `timeout` producer AT ALL. The floor's wall bound is the GitHub Actions job `timeout-minutes` (45, 45, 90 and 5 in the emitted gunbc.witness_floor_workflow), which CANCELS THE JOB and never renders a wrapped command's status as 124; `extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils timeout_command` is consumed only by gunbc.runner_attempt_launch and gunbc.runner_microvm_boot_probe. So on the floor path the ONLY reachable 124 is a subject's own exit 124, and an arm classifying it as environment would fail OPEN on a real subject red -- the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 names as a hard reject, with the harm inverted from the intent that motivated it.",
    "SPECIMEN, gunbc#11814 (side-chat review at 23fefa90, and the producer census this row's author then ran): that PR added exactly such an arm, keyed on the bare status, asserting `GnuCoreutilsTimeoutKill` and annotating that the arm 'is reached only by the exact status GNU coreutils reserves for the timeout path' and 'does not admit real reds'. Both assertions were false, in two independent ways -- the status is not reserved (counterexample above) and on the consuming path it has no timeout producer (census above). The classification was withdrawn and only this row survived. TWO EARLIER REVIEWS ON THE SAME CHANGE had already corrected a forked authority for the constant and a missing failure-mode row, and BOTH APPROVED-OR-MOVED-ON WITHOUT REACHING THIS: the defect was not that 124 was under-documented but that it was over-read.",
    "THE CLASS HAS A LIVE INHABITANT IN EXECUTING CORPUS CODE, AND IT IS NOT THE WITHDRAWN ARM. gunbc.runner_microvm_boot_probe boot_probe_verdict reads `observed.exit_code == coreutils_timeout_expired_exit_code` and returns BootExceededHostDeadline, RENDERING A DEADLINE IT DID NOT OBSERVE -- boot_probe_verdict_text prints `boot=host-deadline-expired after 180 seconds -- the guest did not power itself off and the host killed the VMM`. A Firecracker process that exits 124 for its own reasons produces that sentence verbatim, with a fabricated duration and a fabricated narrative, and the true cause (an ordinary VMM failure, which BootVmmFailed exists to carry) is discarded. This row was first written with the withdrawn classifier as its only site; the live site was named in its own producer census and its conclusion left unstated, which is DESIGN section 4d's third arm -- a fact declined is a wall that does not exist (review 69053).",
    "AND THE DISCRIMINATING FACT A LATER READER WILL REACH FOR, STATED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BECAUSE ONLY ONE OF THEM SURVIVES. It is true that gunbc IMPOSES this deadline itself: runner_microvm_boot_probe_bounded_command wraps the VMM in timeout_command at 180 s with a 10 s kill grace, so unlike the floor path there is a real GNU timeout producer here. That is why this site is the natural place for this row's trigger to be DISCHARGED. It is NOT a defence of the current read: imposing a bound establishes that 124 COULD be the deadline, never that it WAS, because `timeout` returns the managed command's own status when the command exits on its own -- which is exactly what the executed counterexample above does, with the caller imposing a 5 s bound and still receiving 124 at 6 ms. Whether a VMM is LIKELY to exit 124 is an inference about Firecracker and not a fact this corpus holds, so it may not stand in for the observation.",
    "HARM, PER CONSUMER, BECAUSE THE TWO SITES DO NOT SHARE ONE. The withdrawn pure-classifier arm WOULD have misclassified a subject's own exit 124 as environmental, but it never reached the production floor receipt writer -- floor_attempt_receipt_write_stmts branches on 126, 127 and 0 and has no 124 branch -- so no floor behaviour was ever changed, and describing that misclassification in the present tense would be a counterfactual dressed as a reading. The LIVE harm is the boot-probe reader: an ordinary VMM exit 124 can receive a false deadline narrative, with a rendered duration it never observed, while the true cause that BootVmmFailed exists to carry is discarded. Its consequence is CONTAINED and the containment is not the floor's: boot_probe_admitted returns false for BootExceededHostDeadline AND for BootVmmFailed, so both verdicts refuse boot admission and the defect today is a wrong explanation rather than a silenced failure. That containment belongs to this consumer, not to deferred merge gating. A downstream consumer that exempted a misclassified timeout from blocking would additionally silence a subject failure, and that is the state this row exists to prevent being written.",
    "AND THE DUAL THAT REMAINS BEYOND IT, so a later reader does not mistake this row for the whole ambiguity: even where a timeout event IS authoritatively observed, the event still cannot distinguish a subject that was wedged or non-terminating from one that was merely denied CPU on a contended host. That second distinction is real and unresolved; it is simply NOT reachable until the first one is, because asserting the event is the prior step.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- the over-read is writable, no wall rejects it, and two rounds of review passed over it before a third caught it. The observed-vs-inferred distinction this row turns on is DESIGN section 4d's first arm applied to a classifier: do not assert as deduced what is only inferred.",
    "CEILING: structurally guaranteed for the FIRST distinction. Whether a bound fired is a fact the BOUND'S IMPOSER holds at the moment it fires, so a producer that records the event alongside the status makes the over-read unwritable -- the classifier would consume a typed observation rather than re-deriving one from an integer. It cannot reach structural impossibility from the status alone, because the status is a lossy join of two events by upstream's own definition. The SECOND distinction (wedged versus starved) keeps the weaker ceiling: a join against observed progress, never a termination proof, since whether the subject would have finished given more time is the halting question.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, A CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: a typed, EXECUTION-BOUND observation emitted AT THE ACTUAL DEADLINE-ENFORCEMENT DECISION and carried to whatever classifies the outcome. CONSTRUCTING A GNU TIMEOUT INVOCATION DOES NOT ESTABLISH THAT ITS DEADLINE FIRED, and an earlier draft of this row said otherwise: runner_microvm_boot_probe_bounded_command only builds an ArgvCommand through timeout_command, and boot_probe_verdict then receives a ProcessObservation and infers expiry from its exit code, so nothing in this corpus ever observes the enforcement decision. Requesting a bound is not observing that it fired. For an Actions job bound the observation must IDENTIFY TIMEOUT AS THE CANCELLATION CAUSE, since a workflow can also be cancelled by hand and a generic cancellation event would readmit the same ambiguity one layer up. Exit status, elapsed duration and generic cancellation are NOT substitutes -- elapsed duration in particular is the arm gunbc.memory_stall_refusal memory_stall_signal_choice_note refuses, and the 6 ms versus 111 ms separation above is exactly the reading that would license it. The boot-probe route is an INITIAL INTEGRATION SITE for that capability, NOT an existing producer of it. Until such an observation exists, the FLOOR classifier leaves a bare 124 Structural and fails CLOSED, which is the correct disposition there for an unidentified status; that statement is scoped to the floor and explicitly does not describe the boot-probe consumer, which this row already records returning BootExceededHostDeadline off the same bare status.",
  ],
  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils", decl_name: "coreutils_timeout_expired_exit_code", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils", decl_name: "timeout_command", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.ci_failure_class", decl_name: "classify_failure_exit", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.ci_failure_class", decl_name: "floor_attempt_receipt_write_stmts", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.runner_microvm_boot_probe", decl_name: "boot_probe_verdict", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.runner_microvm_boot_probe", decl_name: "runner_microvm_boot_probe_bounded_command", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.memory_stall_refusal", decl_name: "memory_stall_verdict", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
